 "findings": [
  "Most of a seat's deviation from the statewide swing is unpredictable: seats scatter about 3.4 points either side of uniform swing, and the best model cuts that to about 3.2 out of sample. Uniform swing with a handful of adjustments is about as good as it gets.",
  "The sophomore surge is the clearest effect: a first-term member standing again runs about 2.0 points better than the statewide swing (2.4 at state elections, 1.1 federally).",
  "Regression to the mean: for every point of margin a seat starts with, it gives back about 0.07 points (a 10-point seat swings back about 0.7 points against the tide).",
  "Regional seats swing less: they move about 78% of the statewide swing (0.22 points less per point of statewide swing), and over 1999-2025 they drifted about 1.2 points towards the Coalition relative to Melbourne. Much of that regional effect is demographic (it shrinks when census measures are added).",
  "Open seats: a retiring member costs their party about 0.6 points on average at state elections (not significant on its own), more federally (about 1.1).",
  "No momentum: a seat that over-swung last time is no more likely to keep going (if anything it swings back slightly).",
  "Census measures add nothing out of sample once the member and regional effects are in. The one that holds in-sample is the overseas-born share: such seats drift slightly to Labor and amplify the statewide swing (about 9% more swing per standard deviation)."
 ],
 "headline": "Seats scatter ~3.4 pts around uniform swing; sophomores run +2.0 pts ahead, margins regress ~0.07 per point, regional seats move ~78% of the state swing."
}